                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1121,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes out every value where a mask is true, and returns them as one flat list.\n\nThe mask is a tensor of true and false the same shape as the source -\nusually the result of a comparison (<, >, ==). Use it to gather every\nreading above a threshold, every joint inside a region, every pixel of a colour, for counting, averaging or sorting.\n\nTHE RESULT IS FLAT:\nHowever the source is shaped, what comes back is 1-D: the chosen values in reading order (along the first row, then the next). The number that pass is not known in advance and cannot in general be arranged into a rectangle, so the shape is given up. To KEEP the shape - zeros where the mask is false - multiply the source by the mask instead, or use np.where.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.masked_select\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\nsource tensor:\nThe values (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nmask:\nTrue where a value should be taken. Anything non-zero counts as true;\nit is converted to a bool tensor on the source's device. This inlet is cold: the mask is stored and used when the next source arrives, so send it FIRST. Nothing is sent until a mask has arrived. The mask must be the source's shape, or one that broadcasts to it (a single row of 10 applies to every row); otherwise the console prints an error and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselection tensor:\nA 1-D tensor of the chosen values, as many as the mask has true cells. When none are true it is empty (shape [0]).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_disc sends two pictures ten times a second: each cell's distance from the centre, then the numbered 10 x 10 source (tens digit the row, units digit the column). < 0.6 turns the distances into the mask - a white disc - which reaches t.masked_select before the numbers do. The list shows the numbers inside the disc, in reading order, and the strip below draws them as one row. Drag 0.6 on the < node: the disc grows or shrinks, and t.info counts how many cells pass. (Below about 0.16 no cell passes: the list empties, but t.info keeps its last count, because it ignores empty tensors.)\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where keeps the shape and fills the rejected cells from a second source. t.argwhere gives the POSITIONS of the true cells rather than their values. The comparison nodes (greater, less) make masks. t.index_select picks by position.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes out every value where a mask is true, and returns them as one flat list.\n\nThe mask is a tensor of true and false the same shape as the source -\nusually the result of a comparison (<, >, ==). Use it to gather every\nreading above a threshold, every joint inside a region, every pixel of a colour, for counting, averaging or sorting.\n\nTHE RESULT IS FLAT:\nHowever the source is shaped, what comes back is 1-D: the chosen values in reading order (along the first row, then the next). The number that pass is not known in advance and cannot in general be arranged into a rectangle, so the shape is given up. To KEEP the shape - zeros where the mask is false - multiply the source by the mask instead, or use np.where.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.masked_select\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\nsource tensor:\nThe values (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nmask:\nTrue where a value should be taken. Anything non-zero counts as true;\nit is converted to a bool tensor on the source's device. This inlet is cold: the mask is stored and used when the next source arrives, so send it FIRST. Nothing is sent until a mask has arrived. The mask must be the source's shape, or one that broadcasts to it (a single row of 10 applies to every row); otherwise the console prints an error and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselection tensor:\nA 1-D tensor of the chosen values, as many as the mask has true cells. When none are true it is empty (shape [0]).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_disc sends two pictures ten times a second: each cell's distance from the centre, then the numbered 10 x 10 source (tens digit the row, units digit the column). < 0.6 turns the distances into the mask - a white disc - which reaches t.masked_select before the numbers do. The list shows the numbers inside the disc, in reading order, and the strip below draws them as one row. Drag 0.6 on the < node: the disc grows or shrinks, and t.info counts how many cells pass. (Below about 0.16 no cell passes: the list empties, but t.info keeps its last count, because it ignores empty tensors.)\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where keeps the shape and fills the rejected cells from a second source. t.argwhere gives the POSITIONS of the true cells rather than their values. The comparison nodes (greater, less) make masks. t.index_select picks by position."
                },
